{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat memdiag-reader-brief-claims.md && cat memdiag-packet-retention-5.json'","aggregated_output":"# Gate 2 claims reading (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Read only this brief and the packet named in your instructions. Do not open other files or run commands or model calls.\n\nEach entry is one answer of a research step to one article. It lists the **claims** the answer must keep, **stored reference items**\n(how an earlier answer kept them), the **link rule**, the **open theses** the answer could link to, the answer's **new theses**, and its\n**kept evidence** items with the thesis ids each links to.\n\nFor each claim, decide whether the answer keeps it: a kept evidence item states the same figure or event for the same measure as the\nclaim (the item may carry more, and the facts may sit in one item). A detail of the claim (a speaker, a count of companies, a\npercentage) is required only where the stored reference items state it. Then decide whether it is linked as the link rule says.\nJudge by meaning, not by matching words. If `answer_usable` is false, every claim is not kept.\n\nReturn one JSON object per entry, one per line, in packet order, and nothing else:\n`{\"n\": 1, \"claims\": [{\"claim_id\": \"C26\", \"kept\": true|false, \"linked_as_required\": true|false, \"item\": \"<evidence id or null>\", \"why\": \"one sentence\"}]}`\n[\n {\n  \"n\": 73,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n   \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-rack-price\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-bom\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; as a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5-10 percent on the GB200 to 25-30 percent on the VR200 while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-ship-schedule\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter of 2026.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-supply-price\",\n    \"summary\": \"The item reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass the higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-self-sourcing\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per rack.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-price\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 74,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n   \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to a Morgan Stanley Research bill-of-materials analysis cited by MarketScale, Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\",\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's analysis puts memory content for Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million per rack, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage. Memory's share of the bill of materials rose to 25–30% on the VR200 from 5–10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Morgan Stanley-cited bill-of-materials analysis states that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it says AI-buildout demand and laptop memory prices are tied to the same supply squeeze. It flags memory supply tightness and lead times as a scheduling risk for racks that deserves the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 75,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n   \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-rack-price-7-8m\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and the bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; Morgan Stanley says the GPUs themselves are not the driver of the generation-over-generation increase.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-bom-share\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis puts memory content per Nvidia Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory rose from 5-10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25-30% on VR200 while the GPU share fell from roughly 63% to 51%, and Morgan Stanley notes a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-contract-price-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-supply-price-squeeze\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products; it adds that with memory a quarter or more of rack value and supply tight, memory availability and lead times deserve the same planning attention as accelerator allocation.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-contract-price-2027\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-vera-rubin-first-shipments-q3-2026\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's analysis states that first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 76,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10%, while the GPU's share fell from about 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\",\n   \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand has pushed contract DDR5 pricing sharply higher, with device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products, and that memory supply tightness now warrants the same scheduling and planning attention as GPU allocation, with procurement structure able to move rack pricing by $1+ million.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost rather than 5-10%.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was largely absent before; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5-10% on the GB200 to 25-30% on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Per Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is raising component costs across the market, with contract DDR5 pricing having climbed sharply and device makers including Samsung and Apple expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, showing that procurement structure can materially move final pricing by over $1 million per rack.\",\n    \"links\": []\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 77,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n   \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-rack-cost\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the bank adds that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-bom\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; on that basis memory rises from 5 to 10 percent of the GB200 bill of materials to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"memory-contract-price-2026\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-first-shipments\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis, first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-memory-supply\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind rising component costs, that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"memory-contract-price-2026\",\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 78,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n   \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research's bill-of-materials analysis estimates that NVIDIA's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation; the bank estimates NVIDIA will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and says the GPUs are not the driver of the rack price increase.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In Morgan Stanley's analysis, memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is put at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems; memory's share of the bill of materials rose from 5 to 10 percent on GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Morgan Stanley analysis reported by MarketScale states that first shipments of NVIDIA's Vera Rubin rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand is the force behind the higher rack price and is now raising component costs across the market; contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, and device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-1\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the roughly $7.8 million estimate, a reminder that procurement structure materially moves the per-rack number.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-4\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 79,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content per Vera Rubin VR200 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose to 25-30% of the bill of materials from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell to about 51% from roughly 63%.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume. The bank attributes the increase to components other than the GPUs.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to Morgan Stanley's analysis reported by MarketScale, first shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin (VR200 NVL72) racks are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley estimates memory content in Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. On the bill of materials, memory rises to 25-30% on the VR200 from 5-10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share falls from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\",\n     \"clean-3\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"MarketScale reports that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply, with constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand raising component costs across the market, and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products. Memory accounts for a quarter or more of Nvidia's Vera Rubin rack value, so the same squeeze is an input-cost and availability factor for those racks.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the price of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack down to around $6.7 million, meaning procurement structure can move final rack pricing by more than $1 million per unit.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-6\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"n\": 80,\n  \"claims\": [\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M1\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M2\",\n    \"claim\": \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing some memory modules could bring the rack price down to about $6.7 million.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"claim_id\": \"M3\",\n    \"claim\": \"Constrained DRAM, NAND and HBM supply against AI demand has pushed contract memory (DDR5) prices sharply higher (MarketScale's report).\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"stored_reference_items\": [\n   \"In Morgan Stanley's bill-of-materials analysis, memory content per NVIDIA Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack is about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage; memory rose from 5–10% of the bill of materials on GB200 to 25–30% on VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n   \"Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the NVIDIA Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, versus the ~$7.8 million full-sourcing estimate, as procurement structure materially moves the final price.\",\n   \"The MarketScale report cites constrained supply of DRAM, NAND and high-bandwidth memory against AI-driven demand, noting that contract DDR5 pricing has climbed sharply and that device makers including Samsung and Apple are expected to pass higher memory costs into upcoming products.\"\n  ],\n  \"link_rule\": \"No link is required: an empty link counts as linked as required (a statement no open thesis covers is kept without links by design).\",\n  \"answer_usable\": true,\n  \"kept_evidence\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation, and that Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume; the GPUs are not the driver of the increase.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Morgan Stanley puts memory content per Vera Rubin rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more of 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"In the Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials breakdown cited by the article, memory's share of the Vera Rubin VR200 rack bill of materials rose to 25–30% from 5–10% on the GB200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63% to 51%.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"new-memory-prices\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"First shipments of Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth quarter, per the article's account of Morgan Stanley's analysis.\",\n    \"links\": [\n     \"clean-2\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"e5